# Backend conventions

Two nearly identical send implementations — change both when altering email behavior.

## Local — `server/index.js`

- Raw `http.createServer` (not Express)
- Port `3004`
- Routes: `POST /api/send` and legacy `POST /send`; `GET /health`; `OPTIONS` → 204
- Multer memory, **25 MB** / file, max 10 attachments
- HTTP **500** if **all** recipients fail; else 200 with mixed `results`

## Vercel — `api/send.js`

- Express app exported as `module.exports = app`
- Routes registered as `POST /api/send`, `GET /api/health`
- Multer memory, **4 MB** / file (Vercel body limit ~4.5 MB)
- Returns `results` even when all fail (no all-failed 500 special-case)

## Email pattern (both)

```js
// One message per recipient — never CC/BCC
transporter.sendMail({
  from: SMTP_USER,
  to: recipient,
  subject,
  html: body,
  attachments: files.map(f => ({
    filename: f.originalname,
    content: f.buffer,
    contentType: f.mimetype,
  })),
});
```

- Transport: `nodemailer` + `service: 'gmail'`
- Parallel: `Promise.allSettled` over recipients
- Recipients parse: `JSON.parse` first, else comma-split

## When editing

- Keep privacy model (individual To only)
- Prefer moving `SMTP_USER` / `SMTP_PASS` to env vars rather than duplicating literals
- Watch Vercel `maxDuration: 30` for large recipient lists
